WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Session lifetime is a risk and usability decision.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.520
Idle timeout ends a session after a period of inactivity.

3
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.740
Absolute timeout ends a session after a maximum age regardless of activity.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.720
Renewal can extend a session when the user is active or when a token is refreshed.

5
00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:26.420
Remember-me functionality at a high level may intentionally extend convenience, but it should be designed with clear risk controls and user expectations.

6
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.980
Sessions must end predictably when risk changes.

7
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.960
Logout should address the actual authenticated session or token state as appropriate, not merely hide a username or change a button label.

8
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:49.220
Account disablement, password change, MFA reset, suspicious activity, administrative revocation, and privilege changes may all require invalidating or reducing existing session authority.

9
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.760
The application should define these effects before incidents happen.

10
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.240
Revocation is harder in some architectures than others.

11
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.980
Server-side sessions can often be invalidated by changing stored state.

12
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:12.520
Stateless tokens may remain valid until expiration unless the system uses revocation lists, short lifetimes, token versioning, or other invalidation strategies.

13
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:16.780
The right answer depends on the risk, scale, and operational model.

14
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:22.640
The important point is to avoid assuming that logout and revocation work unless the architecture supports them.

15
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.560
Privileged sessions need extra thought.

16
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:37.980
Administrative actions, sensitive exports, credential changes, and security setting changes may justify shorter lifetimes, step-up authentication, stronger monitoring, or separate session state.

17
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.080
Renewal should not silently preserve stale authorization forever.

18
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:52.180
When sessions are renewed, the application should decide what identity, role, tenant, MFA, and risk context are still valid for continued access.
